From b7340f09ae655e797d81fd9a766781ba757f13f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 21:34:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] sealed init: reserve the shadow first, sized to
 the detected address space

The sealed runtime mapped its shadow with MAP_FIXED after two mappings the
kernel had placed wherever it liked, and after asking nothing: sealed init
reads no /proc/self/maps, so patch 0012 had MemoryRangeIsAvailable answer yes
unasked.  Measured, x86_64, the sealed start-up before this:

  mmap(NULL, 65536, ...)                                   = 0x7fb06cafe000
  mmap(NULL, 3727360, ...)                                 = 0x7fb06c770000
  mmap(0x7fff7000, 268435456, ..., MAP_FIXED|...)          = 0x7fff7000
  mmap(0x2008fff7000, 15392894357504, ..., MAP_FIXED|...)  = 0x2008fff7000
  mmap(0x8fff7000, 2199023255552, PROT_NONE, MAP_FIXED|..) = 0x8fff7000

The specification these binaries are verified against admits every placement
the kernel can make, under both mmap layouts and any address-space size, so
a grant inside the future shadow is a run the model contains; and on riscv64
hardware it is a run Linux makes, since the legacy layout's base is
PAGE_ALIGN(2^39 / 3) = 0x2aaaaab000 on every Sv mode -- inside the Sv48
shadow gap -- and `ulimit -s unlimited` selects that layout.  A program whose
first mapping landed there had it covered by the gap and faulted before main.

Two changes, one function each.

SealedReserveShadow runs from __asan_init before InitializeFlags, so before
the runtime's first allocation, and maps the low shadow, the gap and the high
shadow with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, which the kernel refuses with EEXIST where
MAP_FIXED would have replaced.  Each result is accepted only if it is the
address asked for, which also covers a kernel old enough to read the flag as
a hint.  Nothing the runtime maps afterwards can land inside, since the
kernel places an unhinted mapping only in free space; what a program starts
with -- its segments, the stack, the vDSO, the TLS block musl maps -- is a
collision the reservation refuses rather than one it hides, and a refusal at
start-up is the spin of patch 0015.  InitializeShadowMemory then leaves the
three ranges alone, so a sealed start-up makes the same three shadow mmaps,
each with one more flag, first instead of third.  protect_shadow_gap is
pinned to 1 by SealFlags, the gap having been protected before the flag
could be read.

SealedTopOfMemory, riscv64: the sealed runtime pinned Sv48 outright, so that
one binary refused an Sv39 kernel -- and under Sv39 the 14TiB high shadow is
not mappable at all.  It now keeps the constant offset, 0x7fff8000, which is
valid under Sv39 as well (the shadow of a 256GiB space is a 32GiB band above
it), and reads the size of the space off the frame __asan_init started on,
(1 << (msb(frame) + 1)) - 1: 0x7fffffffffff under Sv48 and 0x3fffffffff under
Sv39, the same function of the same thing the unsealed runtime computes, at a
fixed point.  The rule this implements is that the layout is a function of
the initial stack pointer, deterministic in the model, which is what makes
reading the frame admissible where reading /proc was not.  One runtime, one
offset, the same instrumented code, two layouts:

  Sv48, top 0x7fffffffffff                Sv39, top 0x3fffffffff
  [0x10007fff8000, 0x7fffffffffff] HighMem     [0x087fff8000, 0x3fffffffff]
  [0x02008fff7000, 0x10007fff7fff] HighShadow  [0x018fff7000, 0x087fff7fff]
  [0x00008fff7000, 0x02008fff6fff] ShadowGap   [0x0008fff7000, 0x018fff6fff]
  [0x00007fff8000, 0x00008fff6fff] LowShadow   the same
  [0x000000000000, 0x00007fff7fff] LowMem      the same

The arithmetic is held for both tops by static_asserts in sanitizer_sealed.h
-- each region ends before the next begins, HighMem exists, the gap is
exactly the shadow of the shadow -- because no Sv39 machine runs this
project's suite.  kSealedMaxUserVirtualAddress stays the Sv48 constant and
is what SANITIZER_MMAP_RANGE_SIZE is held equal to: it sizes the internal
allocator's bytemap for the largest space, and a detected Sv39 uses a prefix.
x86_64 keeps its constant top, having no Sv39 analogue.

Measured after, x86_64 and riscv64 under qemu-user alike: the three shadow
mmaps come first, MAP_NORESERVE|MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, then the 64KiB and
3727360-byte mappings; a page mapped at 0x80000000 by a constructor ahead of
the sanitizer's own leaves the sealed build spinning with its first
reservation refused EEXIST, where the unsealed build finds the page in its
maps check and dies with the interleave report (x86_64) or probes its shadow
elsewhere and runs (riscv64).  The suite's sealed_collide and
sealed_shadow_layout are those two measurements.
